module

public import Mathlib
public import ChenDalang15.GrowthDefs

/-!
# Stochastic statement layer: white noise, the Walsh integral, random field solutions

Mathlib-only definitions for the stochastic part of Chen–Dalang (2015): the space-time white
noise and its augmented natural filtration (paper, Section 2.2, the paragraph before
`E2:WalshSI`), the predictable class `𝒫₂` (Section 3.1), the Walsh integral characterized
abstractly, and Definition `D2:Solution`.

Nothing here depends on `WalshIntegral`: the white noise, the past/future σ-fields, the
completed natural filtration, the predictable σ-field and `PredL2` are copies (definitions only,
no proofs) of the corresponding `WalshIntegral` v0.7.0 definitions, so that a future Palomar
Challenge can carry them. The Walsh integral itself (`WalshIntegral.Integral.walshL2`) is defined
there by `Classical.choose`; here it is replaced by the predicate `IsWalshIntegral W J`: `J` is a
continuous linear map `PredL2 F P → L²(P)` that agrees with `c · W(1_{(s,t]×A})` on every
elementary integrand `c(ω) 1_{(s,t]}(r) 1_A(y)`. `ChenDalang15.StochBridge` proves that
`walshL2` satisfies the predicate and is the only map that does, so a statement quantified over
all `J` with `IsWalshIntegral W J` is a statement about the Walsh integral.

## Fidelity table for `IsRandomFieldSolution` against `D2:Solution`

The line references below refer to the hash-bound author manuscript recorded in
`docs/PAPER_SOURCE.md`, not to line numbers of the public production source.

Paper: Chen and Dalang, Ann. Probab. 43(6) (2015), doi:10.1214/14-AOP954, Section 2.2 (random-field solution).

| Paper | Lean | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| ll. 459–470: `W` white noise on a complete `(Ω,𝓕,P)`; `𝓕ₜ = σ(W_s(A), s ≤ t) ∨ 𝓝` | `IsAugmentedWhiteNoise P F W` (hypothesis of the headlines, not of the solution predicate) | `P.IsComplete` ∧ `IsWhiteNoise P F W` ∧ `IsCompletedNatural P F W` (augmented natural filtration). `pastSigma W t` is generated by `W h` for `h` essentially supported in `(0,t] × ℝ`, which is `σ(W_s(A), s ≤ t, A ∈ 𝓑_b)` (rectangles generate; `W` is linear and continuous). |
| l. 492: `u = (u(t,x), (t,x) ∈ ℝ₊* × ℝ)` | `u : ℝ → ℝ → Ω → ℝ` | values at `t ≤ 0` are junk: no clause reads them (see below). |
| l. 496 (1): `u(t,x)` is `𝓕ₜ`-measurable for all `(t,x) ∈ ℝ₊* × ℝ` | `adapted : ∀ t > 0, ∀ x, Measurable[F t.toNNReal] (u t x)` | literal. For the augmented filtration this equals "has an adapted version". |
| l. 499 (2): jointly measurable for `𝓑(ℝ₊* × ℝ) × 𝓕` | `jointlyMeasurable : Measurable (fun q : Ioi 0 × ℝ × Ω => u q.1 q.2.1 q.2.2)` | on the subtype `Ioi 0`, so `t ≤ 0` is not constrained. |
| l. 502 (3a): `(G_ν² ⋆ ‖ρ(u)‖₂²)(t,x) < ∞` for `(t,x) ∈ ℝ₊* × ℝ` | `finite : ∀ t > 0, ∀ x, ∫⁻ z in Ioo 0 t ×ˢ univ, ofReal (G ν (t − z.1) (x − z.2)²) * momentFn P (ρ ∘ u) 2 z.1 z.2 < ∞` | lower Lebesgue integral, so no integrability side condition; only `0 < s < t` is read. |
| ll. 477–487: `I(t,x) = ∬ G_ν(t−s,x−y) ρ(u(s,y)) W(ds,dy)`, defined (ll. 1141–1157) for integrands in `𝒫₂` | `IsIntegralField J ν ρ u I`: for `t > 0` there is `g : PredL2 F P` with `g =ᵐ (s,y,ω) ↦ G ν (t−s) (x−y) ρ(u s y ω)` and `I t x = J g` | Integral well-definedness: the paper's integral is defined only on `𝒫₂`, so the clause states well-definedness; it does not shrink the solution class (`P2:Pm-Ext`). `PredL2` = `𝒫₂` by `WalshIntegral.dense_elementary`. `spaceTimeSample` restricts to `s > 0`, and for `ν > 0` (assumed by every headline) `G ν r = 0` for `r ≤ 0`, so only `0 < s < t` is read; for `ν < 0` the formula would read `s > t`. |
| l. 503 (3b): `(t,x) ↦ I(t,x)` continuous from `ℝ₊* × ℝ` into `L²(Ω)` | `ContinuousOn (fun z => I z.1 z.2) (Ioi 0 ×ˢ univ)` | L2 continuity on the positive-time domain. |
| l. 506 (4): `u = J₀ + I` a.s., for all `(t,x) ∈ ℝ₊* × ℝ` | `∀ t > 0, ∀ x, u t x =ᵐ[P] fun ω => J0 ν μ t x + I t x ω` | null set may depend on `(t,x)`, as in the paper. `J0` is a Bochner integral: the headlines assume data for which it converges. |

The Walsh integral `J` fixes the filtration `F` through its type; the solution predicate does not
mention `W`. The headline statements assume `IsAugmentedWhiteNoise P F W` and
`IsWalshIntegral W J`, which pins `F` to the paper's filtration and `J` to the Walsh integral.

